# Project delta for frame workers (read after `_role.md` and your packet; it wins on conflicts)

Project: Vietnamese e-learning lesson "{{TITLE}}", 1920×1080,
rendered with **HyperFrames CLI 0.7.99** (the skill docs describe 0.8.x — where they differ, this file wins).

## Start from the shared skeleton

Copy `tools/frame-skeleton.html` verbatim to your frame's `src` path, then replace:
- `FRAME_ID` → your frame id (the `data-composition-id` your packet/dispatch names, e.g. `08-vong-lap-cua-mot-agent`);
- `PFX` → `f` + your two-digit frame number (e.g. `f08`). **Every element id and class you author starts with `PFX-`** — ids must not start with a digit (`#08-…` is an invalid CSS/GSAP selector; lint `id_requires_css_escape`);
- `DURATION` → your frame's `duration` in seconds (number only), everywhere;
- `HUE` → `{{HUE_BASE}} + {{HUE_STEP}} × chapter number` (ch0 → {{HUE_BASE}}, ch1 → {{HUE_CH1}}, …).
Only the stage content, frame-specific CSS and the timeline body differ between frames. Keep the
`@font-face` block, ground clip, stage box, full-span anchor and the `__timelines` guard.

## 0.7.99 rules (lint errors otherwise)

- `window.__timelines = window.__timelines || {};` must precede the registration (skeleton has it).
- Use `tl.set` for instant state changes (color swaps, show/hide of inner nodes); avoid two tweens on
  the same property of the same element overlapping in time (`overlapping_gsap_tweens`).
- Never tween `.clip` elements themselves (opacity/visibility/autoAlpha) — animate children.
- Never pair a CSS `transform` with a GSAP tween on the same element; set initial state in `fromTo`.
- No `<br>` in text; no `<audio>`/`<video>`; no `Math.random`, `Date.now`, `setTimeout`,
  `requestAnimationFrame`, `repeat: -1`, network fetches.
- CLI, if you ever need it: only `npx -y hyperframes@0.7.99 …`. Never run `hyperframes add`,
  `skills`, `upgrade`, `init`, `lint`, `render` — the orchestrator lints after each wave.

## Timing = the voice

- Your storyboard block has `- cues: word@seconds, …` — aligned, frame-relative times of the keywords.
  Land each reveal of a keyword's visual **on its cue time** (±0.1 s). Scene windows and `shots` are
  already retimed to the real `duration`; follow them. Nothing appears before it is said.
- The karaoke band already shows every spoken word at the bottom (y ≥ 918). On-screen text is short
  motion-graphics copy (1–5 words: the keyword, a number, a label) — never a full narration sentence.
  Exception: real terminal text (commands/output) inside a terminal window.

## Registry references (read-only ideas, not imports)

When your `focal` names a registry block (e.g. `code-terminal-run`, `svg-stroke-trace`,
`constellation-hub`, `count-up`), read `.hyperframes/registry-ref/<name>.html` for the idea and easing,
then **hand-build a seek-safe equivalent on your own timeline**, scaled to your cues. Never mount,
copy-include or `add` those files.

## Facts and text

- On-screen claims, numbers and commands come only from your voiceover, `capture/terminal/*.txt`
  or `capture/extracted/visible-text.txt`. Vietnamese text keeps its diacritics exactly.
- Terminal windows: surface panel, three muted dots, mono title, cyan `$` prompt, typed-on text.

## Self-check before you finish (you cannot run lint)

1. File exists at your `src`; it is exactly one `<template>…</template>`.
2. `data-composition-id` on `#root` and the `__timelines` key both equal your frame id; no element id starts with a digit.
3. `data-duration` on root and both skeleton clips equals your `duration`; every tween ends ≤ duration.
4. Every cue in your `cues` bullet has a visible reveal at that time.
5. Everything stays inside x 80–1840, y 60–880 at every moment (including during motion).
6. No `http` other than the GSAP script tag; no forbidden APIs listed above.
7. Every late `fromTo` has `immediateRender: false`; no vars object is shared between tweens; none of ① ② ③ ✳ ✕ ✓ → appears outside comments.
8. At no moment are about 40 or more elements with `radial-gradient`, `filter: blur()` or `clip-path` on screen (the
   renderer outputs black frames). Bake repeated textures into a PNG under `assets/images/` instead.

## Timeline traps

- A `fromTo` placed late on the timeline applies its from-state at time 0 by default (`immediateRender`), which
  can blank the frame from the start. Give every `fromTo` that does not start at 0, or that follows another tween
  on the same element, `immediateRender: false` in its to-vars, and set the initial state with CSS or `gsap.set`.
- Never reuse one vars object for several tweens (`const v = {…}; tl.to(a, v); tl.to(b, v)`). GSAP mutates
  it, so the later tweens misbehave; write a fresh object literal per tween.

## Glyphs the fonts lack

The shipped fonts have no ① ② ③ ✳ ✕ ✓ →. The browser silently swaps in another font, so these look wrong in
the render. Draw them as inline SVG (circle + digit, spark, cross, tick, arrow), and use plain digits in number
badges. The orchestrator's `frame-guard` rejects these characters outside comments.

## Times of words that are not cues

`cues` lists only the keywords. For any other spoken word, read its exact frame-relative time from
`audio_meta.json` → `voices[]` entry whose `frame` equals your frame number → `words[]`
(`text`, `start`, `end`). Never guess a word time.

## Motion properties

Animate only transforms and paint (`x`, `y`, `xPercent`, `yPercent`, `scale`, `rotation`, `opacity`,
`filter`, SVG `attr`/`strokeDashoffset`). **Never tween `left`, `top`, `width`, `height`, `margin`,
`padding` or `font-size`** — lint `gsap_non_transform_motion` is an error on 0.7.99 (it stutters in the
seek-by-frame capture). To move a dot along a bar, put it in a full-width mover and tween `xPercent`.

## Initial hidden state

Set an element's initial hidden state with CSS (`opacity: 0`) or `gsap.set(...)` **before** building the
timeline — never `tl.set(..., 0)` (a zero-duration set at 0 does not render at frame 0; lint
`gsap_timeline_set_initial_hide`). Entrances use `tl.fromTo`.

## Counting numbers

GSAP parses numeric strings: `textContent: "64.000"` becomes 64. Count integers with
`tl.fromTo(el, {textContent: 0}, {textContent: N, snap: {textContent: 1}, …})` and put separators or
suffixes (".000", "+", "%") in a static sibling span.
